================================================================================
PART 1 - CLIENTSYNTH ADDITIONAL TERMS
================================================================================

ClientSynth generates synthetic people and organizations. Its purpose is to let
a market be tested, demonstrated and understood before real participants exist.
These terms exist so that no real person is ever left unsure whether the party
they are dealing with is real.

1. Definitions

   "Software" means ClientSynth and any Derivative Work of it, as those terms
   are used in the Apache License, Version 2.0.

   "Synthetic Content" means any profile, record, document (including PDFs),
   image, dataset, message, or other material that is generated in whole or in
   part by the Software, or that is derived from such material.

   "Real Participant" means an actual person or organization that takes part
   in a market, marketplace, directory, platform, or service in their own
   right, and that is not itself Synthetic Content.

   "Provenance Marks" means every visible watermark, machine-readable
   watermark, signature, metadata field, and label that the Software applies or
   verifies to identify material as synthetic, together with the code that
   applies, reads, or verifies them.

   "Live Environment" means any system, service, dataset, or event in which
   Real Participants interact with one another, are shown content, are matched,
   scored, contacted, or otherwise affected by what the system presents.

2. No mixing of synthetic and real participants

   You may not make Synthetic Content available in, or import it into, a Live
   Environment, whether simultaneously with Real Participants or in
   combination with their data. In particular you may not:

   (a) populate or seed a marketplace, directory, or matching pool with
       Synthetic Content while Real Participants are present or being
       recruited;
   (b) combine Synthetic Content and records of Real Participants in a single
       pool, ranking, match result, or comparison that is shown to, or acts on,
       any Real Participant; or
   (c) retain a synthetic population in a market after the first Real
       Participant is admitted to it.

   A market that is to serve Real Participants must begin with zero Synthetic
   Content. Configuration, schemas, vocabulary, and code may carry over from a
   synthetic prototype; the synthetic population may not.

3. No presenting synthetic as real

   You may not present, describe, or distribute Synthetic Content in a way
   that could lead a person to believe that it is a Real Participant or a
   real person's or organization's work, record, or testimony. This applies
   whether or not any Real Participant is present, and in particular to
   investors, sponsors, regulators, press, research subjects, and members of
   the public. Wherever Synthetic Content is shown, it must be identifiable
   as synthetic to the person viewing it.

4. Permitted use

   Subject to this license, you may use the Software and Synthetic Content to:

   (a) develop, test, and debug software, including matching and scoring
       mechanisms;
   (b) simulate market behavior and friction;
   (c) demonstrate a market's operation to sponsors, investors, and other
       audiences, provided every participant shown is synthetic and is
       identified as such;
   (d) conduct research on closed datasets in which Synthetic Content keeps its
       Provenance Marks and is never presented to Real Participants; and
   (e) test or evaluate methods for detecting synthetic content, under the same
       conditions as (d).

5. Provenance Marks

   You may not remove, alter, obscure, disable, forge, or circumvent any
   Provenance Mark, nor remove or bypass the code that applies or verifies
   them, in the Software or in any Synthetic Content. A Derivative Work of the
   Software must keep Provenance Marking functional at least as strong as the
   version from which it derives. You may not create a Derivative Work whose
   purpose or effect is to make Synthetic Content indistinguishable from the
   records of Real Participants.

6. Passing these terms on

   (a) Whenever you distribute the Software or a Derivative Work, in source or
       object form, you must include this entire file and require recipients to
       accept it.
   (b) Whenever you give Synthetic Content to a third party, you must give
       them notice of Sections 2, 3, and 5, and you must not remove its
       Provenance Marks.
   (c) You are responsible for compliance by anyone to whom you provide the
       Software or Synthetic Content, or who uses it on your behalf.

7. Termination

   Any breach of Sections 2, 3, 5, or 6 automatically terminates all rights
   granted to you under this license, including the patent license in Section
   3 of the Apache License, as of the moment of the breach. Termination does
   not require notice. The Licensor may, in writing and at the Licensor's sole
   discretion, reinstate your rights. Until it does so, any further use of the
   Software is unlicensed. On termination you must stop making Synthetic
   Content available in any Live Environment and must withdraw it from any
   place where Real Participants could encounter it.

8. Name and marks

   In addition to Section 6 of the Apache License, you may not use the names
   "ClientSynth" or "DeeperPoint", or any Provenance Mark design, to identify
   a Derivative Work that does not comply with Section 5.

9. Interpretation

   These Additional Terms are conditions on the grant of the licenses in
   Sections 2 and 3 of the Apache License. Where a term here is held
   unenforceable it is to be applied to the greatest extent permitted, and the
   remaining terms continue in force. Nothing here limits the Licensor's other
   rights or remedies, including under copyright, contract, and applicable law
   against removal of rights-management information.


